<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sekhukhune I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1814–1882</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sekhukhune</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sekhukhune I was king of the Pedi in the north-east of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> from 1861 to 1879. From his mountain capital at Tsate he resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> South African Republic, whose army failed to defeat him in 1876. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, a British and Swazi force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Garnet Wolseley</a> stormed his stronghold in 1879 and captured him. Released in 1881, he was murdered the following year by his half-brother Mampuru.</p></div>
